L.append(lesson(
 "ci-cd-github-actions","CI/CD & GitHub Actions","Block the newly vulnerable dependency, not the whole history","Use dependency review as a pull-request delta gate.","Security gate · dependency review",
 '''<p>A building inspector checks the new room added by today's permit, not every brick ever laid. GitHub dependency review compares dependency changes in a pull request and can fail when the change introduces a dependency with a known vulnerability.</p><p>The key word is <strong>introduced</strong>. A pull-request gate helps stop new risk from entering. Existing vulnerable dependencies still need an inventory and remediation program.</p>''',
 '''<p>A pull request upgrades <code>widget-lib</code> from 1.4 to 2.0 and adds <code>parser-x 3.1</code>. The dependency review finds a high-severity advisory on parser-x. With <code>fail-on-severity: high</code> and the job marked required, the merge is blocked. A moderate advisory does not cross that chosen threshold.</p>''',
 {"when":"Use dependency review on repositories whose pull requests change supported package manifests or lockfiles and where new vulnerable or disallowed dependencies should block merge.","example":"A Java data-service PR adds a serializer library; the required dependency-review job checks the dependency diff before the service can merge.","decision":"Choose a severity and license policy that matches organizational risk, make the check required, and manage exceptions explicitly. Pair it with scanning and remediation for vulnerabilities already present."},
 "Change the highest severity introduced by the PR. Watch the required check pass or block at the configured threshold.",
 "The browser evaluates four severity labels. It does not call GitHub, parse manifests, query the Advisory Database, model transitive reachability or license policy, or prove that a status check is required. Availability depends on GitHub plan and repository configuration.",
 '''<div id="world"><label>Highest new advisory <select id="depSeverity"><option value="none">none</option><option value="low">low</option><option value="moderate">moderate</option><option value="high">high</option><option value="critical">critical</option></select></label><div id="depWorld" class="world" aria-live="polite"></div></div>''',
 "function paintDep(){const rank={none:0,low:1,moderate:2,high:3,critical:4},s=depSeverity.value,block=rank[s]>=3;depWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"gate '+(block?'fail':'ok')+'\"><strong>dependency-review / policy</strong><br>fail-on-severity: high<br>PR delta: '+s+'<br><strong>'+(block?'BLOCK MERGE':'PASS')+'</strong></div><p>Only the synthetic PR delta is evaluated here.</p>';};depSeverity.onchange=paintDep;paintDep();",
 [q("A PR introduces one high advisory and threshold is high. Result?",["The review job fails","It always passes","The old dependency graph is deleted"],0,"The introduced severity meets the fail threshold."),q("Why make the job a required check?",["A failing job must actually block merge","It makes advisories impossible","It scans production memory"],0,"Enforcement needs branch/ruleset wiring in addition to job logic."),q("What remains outside this delta gate?",["Existing vulnerable dependencies","New dependency changes","The chosen severity threshold"],0,"A PR-delta gate is not a full backlog-remediation program.")],
 [{"code":"severity_rank = {'none': 0, 'low': 1, 'moderate': 2, 'high': 3, 'critical': 4}","note":"Order the supported policy levels."},{"code":"threshold = severity_rank['high']","note":"Configure the merge-blocking threshold."},{"code":"introduced = severity_rank['high']","note":"The synthetic PR adds a high-severity advisory."},{"code":"merge_allowed = introduced < threshold","note":"The required check rejects the change."}], {"merge_allowed":False},
 [["GitHub dependency review","https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/supply-chain-security/dependency-review","Living documentation"],["Configure dependency review action","https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/how-tos/secure-your-supply-chain/manage-your-dependency-security/configure-dependency-review-action","Living documentation"],["Customize dependency review policy","https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/tutorials/secure-your-dependencies/customize-dependency-review-action","Living documentation"]],
 "The gate shows the exact comparison between the PR's highest introduced severity and the configured threshold, separating detection from enforcement.",
 ["What severity and license exception process would your repositories use, and who owns it?","How will you find and reduce vulnerable dependencies that predate the pull-request gate?"]))

L.append(lesson(
 "apis-microservices","APIs & Microservices","Wait for ready, but never wait past the deadline","Queue a gRPC call through transient connection failure without making it unbounded.","gRPC · connection behavior",
 '''<p>An elevator is temporarily between floors. You can leave immediately and take the stairs, or wait—provided you still leave by your appointment time. gRPC's Wait-for-Ready makes the same choice for an RPC created while the channel is in transient failure.</p><p>Without it, the call fails immediately. With it, the call waits for the channel to become ready. The deadline still applies and stops the wait.</p>''',
 '''<p>A batch worker creates <code>ReconcileOrder</code> while the channel is in <code>TRANSIENT_FAILURE</code>. The service recovers after 3 seconds. With a 5-second deadline and Wait-for-Ready, the request is sent with 2 seconds remaining. With a 2-second deadline, it ends before recovery.</p>''',
 {"when":"Use Wait-for-Ready for batch or startup flows where a short transient connection failure should be absorbed and the caller has a meaningful deadline.","example":"A scheduled reconciliation worker starts while the gRPC service is rolling and can wait a few seconds before sending its idempotent request.","decision":"Enable it selectively, always set deadlines, and bound queue/memory pressure. Prefer immediate failure for interactive requests that need fast fallback or when waiting would hide a sustained outage."},
 "Set recovery time and deadline. Watch the RPC remain queued, send with remaining budget, or expire.",
 "This local state model does not create a gRPC channel, model resolver/load-balancer states, backoff, permanent failures, cancellation races, retries, hedging, queue limits, streaming, or language-specific APIs.",
 '''<div id="world"><label>Server ready after <input id="readyAfter" type="range" min="1" max="8" value="3"> seconds</label><label>Deadline <input id="rpcDeadline" type="range" min="1" max="8" value="5"> seconds</label><div id="grpcWorld" class="world" aria-live="polite"></div></div>''',
 "function paintGrpc(){const r=+readyAfter.value,d=+rpcDeadline.value,send=r<d;grpcWorld.innerHTML='<div class=channel><div class=node><strong>t=0</strong><br>TRANSIENT_FAILURE<br>RPC queued</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"node '+(send?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>t='+Math.min(r,d)+'s</strong><br>'+(send?'READY · send<br>'+(d-r)+'s budget remains':'DEADLINE_EXCEEDED<br>never sent')+'</div></div>';};readyAfter.oninput=paintGrpc;rpcDeadline.oninput=paintGrpc;paintGrpc();",
 [q("Ready at 3s, deadline at 5s, Wait-for-Ready enabled. What happens?",["The RPC sends with about 2s remaining","It fails at t=0","The deadline resets"],0,"The call waits, but the original deadline keeps counting."),q("Why keep a deadline?",["It bounds waiting during sustained failure","It guarantees the server recovers","It enables authorization"],0,"Wait-for-Ready without a useful bound can wait too long."),q("When is fail-fast often better?",["An interactive request has a faster fallback","A bounded batch can wait through rollout","The channel is already ready"],0,"User-facing latency and fallback strategy may favor immediate failure.")],
 [{"code":"ready_after_s = 3","note":"The channel will recover after three seconds."},{"code":"deadline_s = 5","note":"The caller's total patience is five seconds."},{"code":"sent = ready_after_s < deadline_s","note":"Wait-for-Ready sends only if readiness arrives before expiry."},{"code":"remaining_budget_s = deadline_s - ready_after_s if sent else 0","note":"Waiting consumes deadline budget; it does not reset it."}], {"sent":True,"remaining_budget_s":2},
 [["gRPC Wait-for-Ready guide","https://grpc.io/docs/guides/wait-for-ready/","Living documentation"],["gRPC deadlines guide","https://grpc.io/docs/guides/deadlines/","Living documentation"],["gRPC service config","https://grpc.io/docs/guides/service-config/","Living documentation"]],
 "The channel timeline uses the same clock for readiness and deadline, making the race and remaining budget visible rather than describing a generic retry.",
 ["Which RPCs in your service may safely wait through startup, and which need fast fallback?","What queue, deadline, and idempotency signals would reveal harmful Wait-for-Ready use?"]))

